WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.040
When you catabolize an amino acid, you face two separate problems.

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:12.160
The carbon skeleton is valuable fuel that can be burned in the TCA cycle or rebuilt

3
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.940
into glucose and fatty acids, but the amino nitrogen is a liability.

4
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.640
Free ammonia is toxic, so the cell splits the molecule.

5
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:24.500
Nitrogen is collected onto glutamate, shuttled safely through the blood as alanine

6
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.000
and glutamine, funneled to the liver, run through the urea cycle, and excreted in

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.700
urine. Carbon can be burned or rebuilt.

8
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:36.640
Nitrogen must be collected, transported safely, and excreted.

9
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.340
Keeping these two fates separate is the organizing logic of the entire chapter.

10
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.990
Stripping nitrogen from most amino acids takes two moves.

11
00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.330
Step 1 is transamination.

12
00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:53.330
An aminotransferase hands the amino group from many different amino acids onto

13
00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:57.990
alpha-ketoglutarate, collecting all that nitrogen onto a single carrier, glutamate.

14
00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:02.930
This step is reversible and funnels diverse inputs into one channel.

15
00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.010
Step 2 is oxidative deamination.

16
00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.930
Glutamate dehydrogenase releases the nitrogen from glutamate as free ammonia,

17
00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.990
regenerating alpha-ketoglutarate to do it all again.

18
00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:19.150
The reaction can run both ways, but in the liver the urea cycle keeps drawing the

19
00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.150
ammonia off, so the nitrogen flows toward disposal.

20
00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:28.670
Many amino acids in, two moves, and free ammonia out, to be packaged as glutamine or

21
00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.050
fed to the urea cycle.

22
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.220
Working muscle has a clever way to ship its nitrogen home.

23
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.300
As muscle breaks down amino acids, it transaminates the nitrogen onto pyruvate to

24
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.160
make alanine. Alanine is non-toxic, so it travels safely through the blood to the

25
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.740
liver. There the liver strips the nitrogen back off, feeding it into the urea cycle,

26
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.560
and converts the leftover pyruvate into glucose.

27
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.560
That glucose returns to the muscle as fuel.

28
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.040
So alanine is the nitrogen shuttle, and glucose is the energy shuttle.

29
00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:16.500
Running in opposite directions.

30
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.900
The muscle offloads its waste nitrogen and gets fuel back in the same elegant round

31
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:22.900
trip.

32
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.720
Glutamine is the workhorse of nitrogen transport and the most abundant free amino

33
00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:28.720
acid in your blood.

34
00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.680
Tissues everywhere add ammonia onto glutamate's side chain to make glutamine, which

35
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.320
carries two nitrogens safely and without toxicity.

36
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.340
The bloodstream then delivers it where it is needed.

37
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.080
The liver for urea synthesis, the kidney for acid-base balance, and rapidly dividing

38
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:50.060
cells like those in the gut and immune system that use glutamine as fuel and as a

39
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:55.160
building block. Ammonia is toxic, so the body keeps it low in the general blood.

40
00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.500
Much nitrogen travels as glutamine, the safe, abundant, two-nitrogen courier.

41
00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:04.473
Here is an analogy that makes the liver's chemistry click.

42
00:03:05.173 --> 00:03:07.533
Picture a bicycle with two wheels that share a chain.

43
00:03:08.113 --> 00:03:12.753
One wheel is the urea cycle, the other is the TCA cycle, and they are physically

44
00:03:12.753 --> 00:03:15.793
linked through shared intermediates like fumarate and aspartate.

45
00:03:16.433 --> 00:03:18.513
When you pedal, both wheels turn together.

46
00:03:19.213 --> 00:03:23.773
Urea comes out one side as the nitrogen waste product, and glucose-building carbon

47
00:03:23.773 --> 00:03:24.773
comes out the other.

48
00:03:25.193 --> 00:03:27.173
The liver runs both wheels at once.

49
00:03:27.813 --> 00:03:32.193
Dispose of nitrogen as urea while reclaiming carbon for energy and glucose.

50
00:03:33.113 --> 00:03:38.193
Nitrogen out one side, glucose out the other, one connected machine doing two jobs.
